  "title": "Word formation",
  "description": "Suffixes and prefixes that build new words: jobs, diminutives, abstract nouns and more.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "Polish builds whole families of words from one root. Knowing the common suffixes and prefixes lets you guess the meaning of a new word.",
      "table": {
        "columns": ["Affix", "Meaning", "Examples"],
        "rows": [
          [
            "-arz, -nik, -ca",
            "a person who does something",
            "piekarz (baker), pracownik (employee), sprzedawca (seller)"
          ],
          ["-ka", "the woman's form of a person noun", "nauczycielka, studentka, Polka"],
          [
            "-ek, -ka, -ko",
            "small or affectionate (diminutive)",
            "dom → domek, pies → piesek, okno → okienko"
          ],
          ["-ość", "an abstract quality", "młody → młodość, wolny → wolność"],
          [
            "-anie, -enie, -cie",
            "the action of a verb",
            "czytać → czytanie, palić → palenie, pić → picie"
          ],
          [
            "-owy, -ny, -ski",
            "an adjective from a noun",
            "dom → domowy, szkoła → szkolny, Polska → polski"
          ],
          ["-o, -ie", "an adverb from an adjective", "szybki → szybko, ładny → ładnie"],
          ["nie-", "not, un-", "miły → niemiły, możliwy → niemożliwy"],
          [
            "prze-, wy-, za-, na-",
            "on verbs: a completed action or a new nuance",
            "czytać → przeczytać, pić → wypić, dzwonić → zadzwonić, pisać → napisać"
          ]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": "One root, many words",
      "examples": [
